  "related_work": "Li, Luo, Wang, and Feng (2025) supply the quantile-specific efficiency calculation and the motivation arc from distributional heterogeneity to response-adaptive allocation; their fixed-horizon, fixed-quantile setup informs the score-energy kernel. Noarov, Fogliato, Bertran, and Roth (2025) supply the clipped strongly convex online-design decomposition for scalar ATE Neyman regret. Howard and Ramdas (2022) supply the stitched empirical-process argument and CDF-to-quantile inversion that determine the LIL benchmark. Mineiro and Howard (2023) show how importance weighting yields time- and value-uniform counterfactual CDF bands under adaptive data collection, while also documenting that validity need not imply useful width. Dai, Gradu, and Harshaw (2023) introduce the finite-population Neyman-regret comparison that motivates the allocation coordinate. Cook, Mishler, and Ramdas (2024) show that adaptive assignment and optional stopping can be reconciled with efficient scalar causal inference, but do not treat a quantile-indexed curve. Simchi-Levi and Wang (2023) formulate a welfare-regret and mean-power Pareto problem; their target and inferential coordinate differ from integrated QTE allocation efficiency and LIL width. Xie, Cao, and Xu (2026) study regret--reward-gap Pareto efficiency for combinatorial bandits, not causal QTE curves or time-uniform bands.",
